WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:06.180
Comedians are usually known for their best bits,&nbsp;
but Chris Farley was so much more than just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:11.160
fat guy in a little coat. This is the story&nbsp;
behind the SNL star that was taken too soon.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:17.340
Undoubtedly, one of Farley's most hilarious bits&nbsp;
is "Fat Guy in a Little Coat." Even in "Tommy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.260
Boy," a movie that's 90 percent Farley schtick,&nbsp;
it's a standout. In a moment when Richard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.520
played by David Spade, grows exasperated&nbsp;
with the buffoonish antics of Tommy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.040
played of course by Chris Farley, Tommy grabs&nbsp;
Richard's tiny blazer, puts it on, and chants

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:40.710
"Fat guy in a little coat.&nbsp;
Fat guy in a little coat."

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.880
It feels like an organic moment, even&nbsp;
improvised, and that's probably because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.920
it came from Farley and Spade messing around&nbsp;
in real life. Spade said during a Reddit "Ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.820
Me Anything" session that Farley would&nbsp;
frequently do the bit. Spade recalled,

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.720
"He would say, 'Davey... turn around,'&nbsp;
and I said, 'If this is Fat Guy in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.200
Little Coat I'm not turning around, it's&nbsp;
not funny anymore.' And he would say 'No,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.160
I've got a whole new thing I'm doing.' And&nbsp;
then I'd turn around, and it would be him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.939
in my Levi jacket, and he would say 'Fat guy&nbsp;
in little coat! Don't you give up on it!'"

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.520
Actor Bob Odenkirk and Chris Farley&nbsp;
were quite the dynamic duo at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:19.200
famed Second City comedy theater. Getting&nbsp;
a jump on any NBC-funded collaboration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.820
Odenkirk and Farley created for the&nbsp;
Chicago stage the character of Matt Foley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.760
the woefully ineffective, perpetually-screaming&nbsp;
motivational speaker who tries to scare kids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.640
straight by warning them they&nbsp;
could wind up just like him.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:41.326
"I am 35 years old, I am divorced, and&nbsp;
I live in a van down by the river."

00:01:41.326 --> 00:01:45.300
The name of the character comes from a&nbsp;
close friend of Farley's. The real Matt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.280
Foley did much better for himself than the&nbsp;
fictional one. He and Farley played rugby&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:54.300
at Marquette University together. He went&nbsp;
on to become a priest in suburban Chicago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.359
and he led Farley's funeral service in 1997.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:03.480
Across his half-decade on "Saturday&nbsp;
Night Live," Chris Farley established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:08.280
a few recurring characters, the strangest&nbsp;
among them was the character Chris Farley.

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:14.220
From 1991 to 1993, Chris Farley headlined&nbsp;
a sketch called "The Chris Farley Show," a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.440
mock talk show in which he held nervous, shaky,&nbsp;
one-on-one conversations with stoic celebrities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.300
like Martin Scorsese and Paul McCartney. The&nbsp;
questions weren't very probing: Farley would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.220
nervously ask the guest if they remembered&nbsp;
hugely monumental moments from their career.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.700
"And you were chained to the&nbsp;
thing and you couldn't get away?"

00:02:33.742 --> 00:02:34.952
"Yeah, yeah. I remember."

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.323
"That was awesome."

00:02:38.323 --> 00:02:42.120
Longtime "SNL" writer Tom Davis came&nbsp;
up with the idea for "The Chris Farley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.660
Show" and developed it with Jim Downey.&nbsp;
They were inspired by Farley's behavior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.380
around the "SNL" offices — he'd ask&nbsp;
them if they remembered a famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.400
sketch from the '70s and then recreate&nbsp;
it for them, right there, by himself.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.540
Davis and Downey then submitted the&nbsp;
sketch's script for consideration as a joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.200
thinking it was too niche. To their&nbsp;
surprise, executive producer Lorne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:06.180
Michaels loved it and placed it into&nbsp;
a 1991 episode hosted by Jeff Daniels.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:10.500
Arguably the most famous Chris&nbsp;
Farley "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.480
sketch was "Chippendales Audition,"&nbsp;
airing in 1990. The bit found two men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.740
in a dance-off to land the last open&nbsp;
spot in the Chippendales dance troupe.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.720
The joke was that the guys were played by&nbsp;
Hollywood heartthrob and "SNL" guest host Patrick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:31.020
Swayze and Farley. Swayze's character won, but not&nbsp;
before Farley careened shirtless around the stage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:37.020
his large stomach on display. It's the first&nbsp;
"SNL" sketch where Farley had the main role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:42.720
and castmate Mike Myers thought it was magical.&nbsp;
He said in the documentary "I am Chris Farley,"

00:03:42.826 --> 00:03:46.966
"After the Chippendales, we were like,&nbsp;
'Oh, this guy's going to be a giant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.966 --> 00:03:48.634
He's going to be a huge star.'"

00:03:48.634 --> 00:03:51.032
Future "Better Call Saul" star Bob 

00:03:51.032 --> 00:03:55.440
Odenkirk was a member of the "SNL" writing&nbsp;
staff at the time, and in his 2022 memoir&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.420
"Comedy Comedy Comedy Drama," he discussed&nbsp;
his distaste for "Chippendales Audition,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:06.120
then and now. He was disappointed that the&nbsp;
sketch got so much attention and praise.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.600
"I know it confirmed Chris's&nbsp;
worst instincts about being funny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.200
which was how he proved his worth —&nbsp;
that getting laughed at was as good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:18.840
as getting a laugh [...] Shame and laughter are&nbsp;
synthesized in the worst way. F— that sketch."

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.620
Farley's co-star Chris Rock shared&nbsp;
a similar sentiment in "The Chris&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:26.340
Farley Show," calling the sketch out&nbsp;
for body-shaming the comic. Rock said,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.520
"There's no comic twist to&nbsp;
it. It's just f—ing mean."

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.400
Two years after his termination, Chris Farley&nbsp;
returned to "Saturday Night Live" as a guest host,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:40.920
and it was not an easy week for anyone. Farley&nbsp;
was supposed to bring along a sobriety coach;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:45.240
but he instead showed up ready to drink.&nbsp;
Apparently, the cast and crew could all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:50.280
tell that Farley was in bad shape. "SNL" boss&nbsp;
Lorne Michaels recalled to Rolling Stone,

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.420
"He was not in shape the way he&nbsp;
had been five years earlier. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:58.020
while I felt good about the show, I&nbsp;
think he was spent by [air time]."

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.300
That episode opened with a twisted, but&nbsp;
apparently honest, sketch in which Michaels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.700
is concerned that Farley is too unstable to host&nbsp;
the show. Cast member Tim Meadows reassures him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.580
claiming that Farley has gotten&nbsp;
better. Farley then shows up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.500
and to prove his mettle, introduces&nbsp;
his sobriety sponsor: Chevy Chase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:21.720
another former "SNL" cast member who also&nbsp;
struggled mightily with substance abuse.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:25.200
Later in the show, after Farley&nbsp;
grew visibly winded during a sketch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:29.340
Norm Macdonald struck out with a "Weekend&nbsp;
Update" joke. He brushed it off and said,

00:05:29.468 --> 00:05:35.868
"That one wasn’t very good, but… Try to laugh anyway because it helps Farley have a little rest."

00:05:36.406 --> 00:05:41.866
That "SNL" episode turned out to be the last&nbsp;
time Farley would ever perform for an audience.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:49.260
Farley was a big guy, but he carried himself on&nbsp;
screen and on the "SNL" stage with the grace of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:54.480
athlete, or even a dancer. That made his physical&nbsp;
comedy and stunt work all the more impressive.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:59.400
It's probably not too much of a surprise,&nbsp;
then, to learn that young Chris Farley was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:03.720
extremely talented and accomplished athlete.&nbsp;
He was on the heavier side as a child and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:06.660
despite not having the body one&nbsp;
would usually associate with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:11.940
swimmer, he was positively dolphin-esque.
His brother Tom Farley said to Rolling Stone,

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.720
"Chris would get up on the blocks as&nbsp;
a 10-year-old and kids would start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.920
snickering at him. Then he'd hit the water&nbsp;
with those big, broad shoulders of his,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:25.680
go into the butterfly stroke, kicking up this&nbsp;
rooster tail, and leave those kids in the dust."

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.420
Chris Farley did have the traditional build&nbsp;
of a football player, and as a high schooler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:35.340
in Madison, Wisconsin, he made the all-city team&nbsp;
as a defensive lineman. He had a dream of going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:40.740
pro after school, but when he graduated&nbsp;
he realized that at 5'9" and 230 pounds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.100
he probably wouldn't be big enough&nbsp;
to be an effective NFL player.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:48.720
Beneath the humor, Chris Farley&nbsp;
often played sensitive, hurt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:54.600
and even broken characters. Farley also lent a&nbsp;
boisterous energy to almost every role he played,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:59.280
and when the cameras weren't rolling, he could&nbsp;
apparently be just as wound up as the characters.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.720
On an episode of "The Late Late Show with&nbsp;
James Corden," David Spade recalled that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.740
when the cast of "Tommy Boy" arrived for&nbsp;
filming in Toronto, Farley felt under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.640
weather and went to bed early, so Spade went&nbsp;
out to grab a drink with co-star Rob Lowe.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:17.340
Farley heard about that innocent friend date,&nbsp;
and apparently, he was the extremely jealous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:21.900
type. Spade explained that upon returning&nbsp;
to set, Farley snidely asked him about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:26.760
his hangout with Rob Lowe. The jealousy&nbsp;
then greatly escalated later that day.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.959
Spade recalled that, during lunch, Farley walked&nbsp;
up to him and purposefully stepped on his hand.

00:07:31.959 --> 00:07:35.460
"And then I got up and I threw my Diet Coke&nbsp;
on him. It was straight out of 'Atlanta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:41.122
Housewives’ or something. And then he threw me down the stairs, and then they said 'action.'"

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:44.520
Chris Farley was a religious&nbsp;
guy throughout his lifetime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:48.480
raised in the Roman Catholic faith.&nbsp;
He was educated in parochial schools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:52.140
according to The Bozho, attending the&nbsp;
Catholic-affiliated Marquette University.

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:56.640
In his adult life, that spirituality&nbsp;
manifested as a commitment to community&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.640 --> 00:08:01.620
service. Farley did a lot of hands-on&nbsp;
charity work, even after he ascended to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:05.820
stardom as one of the most famous and&nbsp;
recognizable comedians on the planet.

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:10.260
According to the documentary "I Am Chris&nbsp;
Farley," in his days as a performer with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:14.100
the Second City improv troupe, he'd&nbsp;
never accept payment for charity shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.100 --> 00:08:17.820
saying that his salary should go to&nbsp;
whatever charity the performance benefitted.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:24.000
After he moved to New York for "SNL," Farley&nbsp;
frequently volunteered at shelters and unhoused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.040
meal centers, and he also made visits to&nbsp;
pediatric wards at local hospitals. Most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:33.960
his closest friends didn't even know that Farley&nbsp;
did all that stuff until long after he died.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:39.360
Farley struggled with addictions to drugs&nbsp;
and alcohol, and he sadly lost that war&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:44.280
on December 18, 1997. The comedian-actor&nbsp;
was discovered in his Chicago apartment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:50.100
after reportedly going on a four-day bender.&nbsp;
But Farley wasn't exactly a reckless junkie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.960
or a live fast/die young kind of guy&nbsp;
— he desperately tried to get clean.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:09:01.380
In just the last two years of his short life,&nbsp;
Farley entered rehabilitation facilities 17 times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:06.360
which is to say nothing of other attempts at&nbsp;
a healthier life, such as quitting substances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:12.300
cold turkey, weight-loss programs, joining&nbsp;
Alcoholics Anonymous and Overeaters Anonymous, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:17.880
interventions from friends and coworkers. Farley&nbsp;
even checked himself into the famous Hazelden drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:23.220
rehab center in Minneapolis just before his death,&nbsp;
but he always struggled to fully quit for long.

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:25.920
"You're gonna die from that&nbsp;
s–t, buddy. You gotta stop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:32.460
you gotta stop. It's not gonna&nbsp;
end right, and…there you go."

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:36.840
That commitment extended to movie&nbsp;
sets. The last film Farley worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:41.460
on was the adventure comedy "Almost Heroes."&nbsp;
During the first week of shooting in 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:45.600
Farley was drinking so much that when&nbsp;
producers found out, they threatened to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.280
shut the movie down. That led Farley to hire&nbsp;
a sobriety guard, who hung out with him for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.720
the duration of the shoot, making sure&nbsp;
he didn't ingest any illicit substances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.982
Producer Denise Di Novi even found Alcoholics&nbsp;
Anonymous meetings for the actor to attend.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.480
Prior to his death, Chris&nbsp;
Farley completed his work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:07.260
on "Almost Heroes" and had several&nbsp;
potential movies waiting for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:11.520
but there was one other one for which he'd&nbsp;
almost finished his work — and it could've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:17.220
combined his fantastic gifts for comic yelling&nbsp;
and playing sensitive, misunderstood characters.

00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:22.740
Farley had been cast in the title role&nbsp;
in "Shrek," and he'd recorded about 85&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:27.240
percent of his lines. Production held up for a&nbsp;
year while the filmmakers decided what to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:32.460
even considering employing a Farley sound-alike&nbsp;
to record the final 15 percent of the dialogue.

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:37.680
Finally, producers decided to recast&nbsp;
and start over with a brand-new actor:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:43.380
Mike Myers, Farley's old "Saturday Night Live"&nbsp;
castmate. Myers' interpretation of the character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.100
which became the foundation of&nbsp;
a blockbuster "Shrek" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:52.740
was very different from Farley's, as leaked&nbsp;
rough footage later showed. Farley's take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:58.920
was much more understated and even dramatic than&nbsp;
Myers' broadly comic, Scottish-accented version.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:05.280
Chris Farley was among the biggest comedy&nbsp;
stars in the world when he died in 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:10.860
and he was young — just 33 years old. He&nbsp;
likely had a long career in front of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:14.880
and his death brought an end to a&nbsp;
number of intriguing projects that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:19.620
could have shown Farley was capable of a&nbsp;
lot more than just broad, physical comedy.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.580
He had an interest in starring in a film&nbsp;
based on Mordecai Richler's cult-favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:28.260
humor novel "The Incomparable Atuk," a&nbsp;
satire about an Inuit who leaves Alaska&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:32.280
for the first time and experiences&nbsp;
culture shock in New York City.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:36.300
Farley also nearly starred in a&nbsp;
movie version of "A Confederacy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:40.260
Dunces," the Pulitzer Prize-winning&nbsp;
classic novel about an arrogant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:45.000
overeducated loser living amongst&nbsp;
colorful characters in 1960s New Orleans.

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:49.620
It's safe to say Farley could have even&nbsp;
scored himself some Oscar buzz for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:53.820
project he had in the works; the starring&nbsp;
role in a movie about old-time Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:59.160
star and scandal magnet Fatty Arbuckle,&nbsp;
with a script written by David Mamet.

